---
description: Never hide a tracked file from git with assume-unchanged or skip-worktree
alwaysApply: true
---

# Do not hide a file from git

`git update-index --assume-unchanged` and `--skip-worktree` do not quiet a
noisy file. They make `git status`, `git diff`, **and `git add`** ignore real
edits with no warning, so work looks committed when it is not.

Do not set either bit, and do not suggest one to clean up a dirty tree.

| Bit | `git ls-files -v` | Survives `--really-refresh` | Clear with |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `assume-unchanged` | lowercase `h` | no — a differing file loses the bit | `git update-index --no-assume-unchanged <path>` |
| `skip-worktree` | `S` | yes | `git update-index --no-skip-worktree <path>` |

A normal tracked file is `H`, so one line audits a checkout:

```sh
git ls-files -v | grep -v '^H '
```

```powershell
git ls-files -v | Where-Object { $_ -cnotmatch '^H ' }
```

The match is case-sensitive on purpose: `h` is the bit, `H` is healthy.

## Instead

| Goal | Do this |
| --- | --- |
| Keep a generated file out of git | `.gitignore`, plus `git rm --cached` once |
| Local-only configuration | commit a sample; ignore the real file |
| Park edits | `git stash` or a branch — both stay visible |
| Quieter output | `git status --short`, or a pathspec |

If you find a flagged file, clear the bit, show the diff, and say so. On
2026-09-10 an `h` bit had been swallowing a draft and three documents in one
umbrella for days: `git add` reported success and staged nothing.
